[Event "Strategy: the outpost, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/OmJykCAg"]
[FEN "2qr1r1k/p3b1pp/1p1p1p2/1Pp2P2/P1P1P1PP/5Q2/8/2N1R1RK w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2qr1r1k/p3b1pp/1p1p1p2/1Pp2P2/P1P1P1PP/5Q2/8/2N1R1RK_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Today we think like a general: plans, not tricks. First plan, the outpost: a square no enemy pawn can ever attack. Walk your knight to e6. [%csl Ge6][%cal Yc1d3,Gd3f4,Gf4e6] }
1. Nd3 { The knight sets off for e6 by way of f4. None of his pawns can ever attack either square. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gd3f4,Gf4e6] } (1. Ne2 { Also good: the same trip by e2, then f4, then e6. Today we walk the d3 road. [%cal Ge2f4] } 1... Rde8 2. Nf4 { The knight reaches f4 all the same, one hop from e6. [%csl Gf4] }) (1. g5 { A pawn storm that also wins, but f4 was free for the knight right now. } 1... g6 2. Nd3 { After the storm the knight still heads for f4, a move later. [%cal Gd3f4] }) (1. h5 { The h pawn runs ahead alone, and one pawn move stops it. } 1... h6 { Your pawn on h5 is stuck and the knight has not moved. Still better, but slower. [%csl Rh5] }) (1. e5 { A pawn push with nothing behind it. } 1... dxe5 { He takes the free pawn on e5. A pawn down, and the knight never set off. [%csl Re5] }) 1... Rde8 { He doubles his rooks on the e column. Knight to f4 next. [%cal Yd3f4] } 2. Nf4 { From f4 the knight is one hop from e6, and none of his pawns can reach either square. [%csl Gf4,Ge6][%cal Gf4e6] } (2. g5 { Also good: the storm opens the g column, and the knight reaches f4 two moves later. } 2... fxg5 3. hxg5 Bd8 4. Nf4 { The knight is on f4 after all, with the g column open too. [%csl Gf4] }) (2. Rd1 { A rook on the open d column is useful, but the knight's trip comes first. } 2... g5 3. fxg6 hxg6 4. Nf4 { The knight still gets to f4, and you are still clearly better. [%csl Gf4] }) (2. Kg2 { A waiting move. Nothing is lost, but the knight is no closer to e6. } 2... Qd7 3. Nf4 { The knight reaches f4 a move late, and you are still clearly better. [%csl Gf4] }) 2... Bd8 { His bishop steps back to d8. Now the last hop: knight to e6. [%cal Yf4e6] } 3. Ne6 { Home. Your f pawn guards the knight on e6, and none of his pawns can ever attack it. That is an outpost. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gf5e6] } (3. Rd1 { The open column is nice, but the knight was one hop from home. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd5 Kg8 { Still clearly better, but e6 is still empty. [%csl Re6] }) (3. g5 { Another pawn storm, and the knight lands on d5 instead of e6. } 3... fxg5 4. hxg5 Qa8 5. Nd5 { A knight on d5 is an outpost too, and you are still clearly better. [%csl Gd5] }) (3. Rg2 { A rook shuffle while the knight waits on f4. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd2 Bc7 { His bishop found c7, e6 is still empty, and you are better but slower. [%csl Re6] }) (3. Kg2 { A king step spends the move the knight wanted. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd1 Bc7 { You are still better, but the knight never made its last hop. }) (3. Qd3 { The queen aims at d6, a different plan from the outpost. } 3... Qd7 4. h5 h6 { Still better: e6 and d5 both wait for the knight. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, Fischer's knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/LGsz0l4h"]
[FEN "1r2kb1r/1bq3pp/p4pn1/2p1p3/NpP1P3/1P1QBN2/1P3PPP/2RR2K1 w k - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2kb1r/1bq3pp/p4pn1/2p1p3/NpP1P3/1P1QBN2/1P3PPP/2RR2K1_w_k_-_0_1"]

{ Outposts again. Find a square deep in his camp that none of his pawns can attack, and send a knight there. }
1. Nb6 { The knight hits his queen and eyes d5. His queen cannot take it: after queen takes b6, your queen mates him on d7. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gb6d5,Rd3d7] } (1. Ne1 { The knight heads for d5 by d3, but that road is slow. } 1... Be7 2. Qe2 O-O 3. Nd3 { You got there in the end, but he has castled. About level. [%csl Rd3] }) (1. h4 { This pokes his knight on g6, but your knight on a4 is the piece with no job. } 1... Be7 2. h5 Rd8 { He grabbed the d column, and your knight is still on the rim. About level. [%csl Ra4] }) (1. Nxc5 { The pawn on c5 is guarded, and this costs a piece. } 1... Bxc5 2. Bxc5 Qxc5 { The bishops traded, and you gave a knight for a pawn. He is better. [%csl Rc5] }) (1. Qc2 { A quiet move, and the outpost plan never starts. } 1... Bd6 2. Rd2 Be7 { Your knight is still on a4 doing nothing, and the position is level. [%csl Ra4] }) 1... Rd8 { He challenges the d column. What does your knight on b6 want next? } 2. Nd5 { Home: the knight sits on d5, and none of his pawns can ever attack it. Every piece of his now has to work around it. [%csl Gd5] } (2. Qxd8+ { Also good: the rooks come off first, and the knight still lands on d5. } 2... Qxd8 3. Rxd8+ Kxd8 4. Nd5 { The same knight on the same square, with fewer pieces left. [%csl Gd5] }) (2. Qc2 { The queen leaves d3, and your knight on b6 loses its guard. } 2... Rxd1+ 3. Rxd1 Qxb6 { His queen took the knight on b6, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rb6] }) (2. Qe2 { The queen steps to e2 and stops watching b6. [%csl Rb6] } 2... Rxd1+ 3. Rxd1 Qxb6 { The loose knight on b6 fell to his queen. A piece down. [%csl Rb6] }) (2. Qd7+ { A check that gives the queen away. } 2... Rxd7 3. Nxd7 { A queen for a rook. You are lost. [%csl Rd7] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, a home for the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/8QEYZaHM"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/1b3ppp/p1n1p3/2ppPn2/1p3P2/2PP1NP1/PP2N1BP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 11"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/1b3ppp/p1n1p3/2ppPn2/1p3P2/2PP1NP1/PP2N1BP/R1BQK2R_w_KQkq_-_3_11"]

{ Your knight on f5 sits on an outpost. He would love to push his g pawn to g4 and kick it. }
11. O-O { He castles, and g4 is next. How do you keep your knight on f5? [%csl Rg4] } 11... h5 { Your h pawn now guards g4. If he ever pushes g4, you take it, and the knight stays on f5. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh5g4][%tail] } (11... bxc3 { You open the b column, but his rook gets there first. } 12. bxc3 h5 13. Rb1 { His rook took the b column. About level. [%csl Rb1] }) (11... c4 { Too early: now g4 comes with a hit on your pawn chain. } 12. g4 cxd3 13. Qxd3 { Your knight on f5 must move, his pawns roll, and he is a little better. [%cal Rg4f5] }) (11... g6 { This pawn guards f5 but not g4, so he can still kick the knight. [%csl Rf5] } 12. g4 Ng7 { The knight is pushed back to g7, and he is a little better. [%csl Rg7] }) (11... Be7 { Development is fine, but g4 comes first. } 12. g4 Nh4 13. Nxh4 Bxh4 { Your knight was traded off and the outpost is gone. He is a little better. [%csl Rf5] }) (11... Nh4 { The knight jumps to h4, where it can simply be taken. } 12. Nxh4 { A knight lost for nothing: his knight took it, and the g3 pawn could have too. [%cal Rg3h4] }) 12. a3 a5 13. h3 Be7 { Your bishop is on e7 and you are ready to castle. His g pawn still cannot kick the knight on f5: on g4 your h pawn takes it. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh5g4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, keep the outpost"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/5ppp/3p4/2nPpb2/2R1N3/1p4P1/1P2PPBP/1Q3RK1 w - - 0 24"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/5ppp/3p4/2nPpb2/2R1N3/1p4P1/1P2PPBP/1Q3RK1_w_-_-_0_24"]

{ New plan: the right trade. When pieces come off, pick the trade that keeps your best piece on the board. Your knight on c5 is your best piece. [%csl Gc5] }
24. Qc1 { His queen steps to c1. His knight on e4 is the one piece that can ever trade off your knight. Take it with your bishop. [%cal Yf5e4] } 24... Bxe4 { Your bishop takes his knight. Nothing of his can ever swap off your knight on c5 now. Watch. [%csl Gc5][%tail] } (24... Na6 { The knight leaves c5, and the plan goes with it. } 25. Nd2 Nc5 26. Qc3 { Your knight came back to c5, but his knight escaped to d2. Level. [%csl Rd2] }) (24... Nxe4 { The wrong knight trades: yours on c5 was the best piece. } 25. Bxe4 Bxe4 26. Rxe4 { Everything is traded, and his rook on e4 is the active piece. Level. }) (24... Na4 { The knight wanders to the rim. } 25. Nd2 Nc5 26. Qc3 { Two moves to go nowhere: your knight is back on c5 and his has left e4 for d2. Level. }) 25. Bxe4 f5 { Your f pawn kicks his bishop away. Your knight on c5 against his bishop, and his b2 pawn is fixed and weak: that is the right trade. [%csl Gc5,Rb2][%cal Gf5e4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, the stonewall bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/lPzDl6I7"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp4pp/2pbpn2/3p1p2/2PP4/1P3NP1/P3PPBP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 0 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk2r/pp4pp/2pbpn2/3p1p2/2PP4/1P3NP1/P3PPBP/RNBQ1RK1_b_kq_-_0_7"]

{ His dark bishop on d6 guards every hole in his camp. Which of your pieces can challenge it? [%csl Rd6] }
7... O-O { He castles, and his bishop on d6 still guards the dark squares. [%csl Rd6] } 8. Ba3 { Your bishop offers the trade on a3. Once his bishop on d6 is gone, e5 and c5 have no guard. [%cal Ga3d6][%csl Ge5,Gc5] } (8. Nc3 { A developing move, but his bishop on d6 stays and guards everything. } 8... Qe7 9. e3 Ne4 { His knight sits on e4, he is comfortable, and it is about level. [%csl Rd6] }) (8. Bb2 { The bishop hides behind the d4 pawn and hits nothing. } 8... Nbd7 9. Qc2 Ne4 { An ordinary game: his bishop on d6 was never challenged. [%csl Rb2] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but his bishop on d6 is still there to fight it. } 8... Nbd7 9. Nd3 Qe7 { Your knight went back to d3, and his bishop still rules the dark squares. }) (8. e3 { Also fine: the trade on a3 can still come next move. } 8... Bd7 9. Ba3 Bxa3 10. Nxa3 { The same trade, one move later. [%csl Ga3] }) 8... Bxa3 { He takes on a3. Only one piece can take back. } 9. Nxa3 { The knight takes back. On a3 it is doing nothing yet. } (9. Qc2 { This leaves his bishop on a3 alive. } 9... Bd6 { His bishop went home to d6, and you are a whole piece down. [%csl Rd6] }) 9... Qe7 { His queen takes over the dark diagonal. Where does your knight on a3 belong? } 10. Nc2 { The knight heads for d3 by c2 and e1. On d3 your c and d pawns shield it, and it eyes e5 and c5. [%cal Gc2e1,Ge1d3][%csl Gd3][%tail] } (10. Qc1 { Also good: your queen takes over the diagonal from a3 to f8. } 10... Nbd7 11. Qb2 { The queen on b2 does the dark bishop's job now. [%cal Gb2f6] }) (10. Nb1 { Back home, then d2: a slower road. } 10... Nbd7 11. Qc1 e5 { He freed his position with e5 while you regrouped. About level. }) (10. c5 { The pawn goes forward and becomes a target. } 10... b6 11. b4 a5 { He hits your pawns at once, and the game is level. [%csl Rc5] }) (10. b4 { His queen on e7 simply takes this pawn. } 10... Qxb4 { A pawn gone for nothing, and he is a little better. [%csl Rb4] }) 10... Nbd7 11. Rc1 b6 12. Nce1 Ba6 13. Nd3 { The knight is home on d3, shielded by your c and d pawns. It eyes e5 and c5, and his dark bishop is gone. [%csl Gd3,Ge5,Gc5] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, keep his weaknesses"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/tPkvSFOq"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/pp2p1pp/2p3b1/3np3/8/2P1B2P/PP1NBPP1/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/pp2p1pp/2p3b1/3np3/8/2P1B2P/PP1NBPP1/R2QK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_1"]

{ His e pawns are doubled on e7 and e5, and they get weaker as pieces come off. Which trade do you want? }
1. Bh5 { Your bishop offers a trade for his bishop on g6, his best piece. [%cal Gh5g6] } (1. h4 { A slow pawn push, and his knight takes your bishop on e3. } 1... Nxe3 2. fxe3 Bf7 { He has the two bishops, your e pawns are doubled too, and it is about level. [%csl Re3] }) (1. O-O { Safe, but he gets time to tidy up. } 1... Bf5 2. Bg5 g6 { His bishop left g6 for f5, and he is comfortable. About level. }) (1. Bg5 { This hits his queen, and the trade you wanted waits. } 1... Qc7 2. Bh5 Bxh5 3. Qxh5+ g6 { The bishops trade, but your queen gets pushed back. Level. }) (1. Nc4 { The knight eyes e5, and his queen guards it. } 1... Qc7 2. Bh5 Bxh5 3. Qxh5+ g6 { The trade happens two moves late, and the knight on c4 did nothing. Level. }) (1. Bf3 { This attacks his knight, but his knight takes your bishop first. } 1... Nxe3 2. fxe3 e6 { Your pawns are the broken ones now, and he is a little better. [%csl Re3] }) 1... e6 { He pushes e6 and keeps his bishop on g6. What now? } 2. Bxg6+ { Take it: his best piece is gone, and it was the one guarding his light squares. [%csl Rg6][%tail] } (2. Bf3 { You back off, and your bishop on e3 hangs. } 2... Nxe3 3. fxe3 Qh4+ { Your centre is wrecked and your king is in the open. He is much better. [%csl Re3] }) (2. Ne4 { A jump into the middle, and the trade still comes. } 2... Qh4 3. Bxg6+ hxg6 { You got the trade anyway, and you are a little better. }) (2. O-O { You castle first, and his queen takes back on g6 instead of a pawn. } 2... Qf6 3. Bxg6+ Qxg6 { His queen lands on g6, his pawns stay healthy, and you are only a little better. }) 2... hxg6 3. O-O { Your king is safe on g1. His pawns are doubled twice now, on e6 and e5 and on g7 and g6, and he has no light bishop left. [%csl Re6,Re5,Rg7,Rg6] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/6x6tlAtk"]
[FEN "r3nrk1/1q3ppp/p1b1pb2/8/1PB2B2/P1N3Q1/5PPP/2RR2K1 b - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3nrk1/1q3ppp/p1b1pb2/8/1PB2B2/P1N3Q1/5PPP/2RR2K1_b_-_-_0_21"]

{ New plan: improve your worst piece. Your knight on e8 does nothing. Open g7 for it with the pawn to g6. [%csl Re8][%cal Yg7g6,Ge8g7,Gg7f5] }
21... g6 { The pawn opens g7 for the knight. From there it can reach f5. [%cal Ge8g7,Gg7f5][%csl Gf5] } (21... Be7 { The bishop steps back, and the knight on e8 still sleeps. } 22. Bf1 Rc8 23. h3 Nf6 { The knight gets out by a slower road, and he is a little better. [%csl Re8] }) (21... Kh8 { A waiting move with the knight still asleep on e8. } 22. Bf1 Be7 23. Be5 { His bishop grabs e5 first, and he is a little better. }) (21... Rd8 { A pair of rooks comes off, and the knight still has nowhere to go. } 22. Bf1 Rxd1 23. Rxd1 Be7 { Nothing is solved: the knight on e8 is still stuck, and he is a little better. }) (21... Rc8 { The rook moves, and the knight keeps waiting. } 22. Bf1 Be7 23. Rd4 { His rook takes d4 first, and he is a little better. }) 22. Bg5 { His bishop hits yours on f6. Keep to the plan: bishop to g7. [%cal Yf6g7] } 22... Bg7 { The bishop steps to g7, and the knight on e8 can jump to f6. [%cal Ge8f6] } (22... Bh8 { Also good: the bishop tucks into the corner, and the knight still gets out. } 23. Qf4 Bg7 24. Qh4 Nc7 { The knight came out by c7 instead. About the same. }) (22... Bxg5 { You give up the bishop, but the knight gets out to g7. } 23. Qxg5 Ng7 24. Bd3 Rfd8 { The knight on g7 eyes f5, and it is about the same. [%cal Gg7f5] }) (22... Rc8 { A rook move with your bishop still attacked. } 23. Bd3 Bxg5 24. Qxg5 Ng7 { The bishops came off, the knight reached g7, and he is a little better. }) (22... h5 { This weakens your king side for nothing. } 23. Bd3 Rd8 24. Bxf6 Nxf6 { Your bishop is gone and your king side has holes. He is better. [%csl Rh5] }) 23. Bf1 { His bishop steps back. Now the knight jumps. [%cal Ye8f6] } 23... Nf6 { The knight is awake. From f6 it reaches d5 and e4, and it is a useful piece at last. [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf6d5,Gf6e4] } (23... h6 { Also good: kick his bishop first, then the knight jumps. } 24. Bf4 Nf6 { The knight lands on f6 one move later. [%csl Gf6] }) (23... h5 { Your pawn moves up, and the knight could have jumped already. } 24. h3 Nf6 { The knight still lands on f6. About the same. }) (23... Rc8 { A rook move, and his bishop reaches h6. } 24. Qh4 Nc7 25. Bh6 { He trades off your good bishop on g7, and he is a little better. [%csl Rh6] }) (23... Bf6 { This offers the very trade you were avoiding. } 24. Bxf6 Nxf6 25. Qe5 { Your bishop is gone and his queen sits on e5. He is better. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, the sleeping rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/r6CnOaY7"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/pp4p1/7p/3p4/P1pPp3/2P1P3/1Q3PPP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/pp4p1/7p/3p4/P1pPp3/2P1P3/1Q3PPP/R1B2RK1_w_-_-_0_18"]

{ The worst piece again. Find your sleepiest piece and give it a job. }
18. Qb5 { His queen comes to b5, eyeing b7 and d5. Which of your pieces has no job? } 18... a5 { The a pawn steps up, and your rook on a8 gets a door: out to a6, then across to g6. [%cal Ga8a6,Ga6g6] } (18... a6 { Also good: the same door, one square lower. } 19. Qb1 a5 20. Ba3 Rf7 { The rook comes out next move either way, and you are clearly better. [%csl Ga8] }) (18... Rf7 { The other rook lifts first, and his a pawn shuts the door on a8. } 19. a5 Bg4 20. Ba3 { Your rook on a8 is stuck behind the a7 pawn. You are better, but less so. [%csl Ra5] }) (18... Bg4 { The bishop comes out, but the rook on a8 still has no road. } 19. Ba3 Rf7 20. Qc5 { His pieces woke up first, and you are only a little better. }) (18... Rf6 { The f rook wanders sideways, and the a rook still sleeps. } 19. Ba3 Kh7 20. Qb1 { His bishop is out on a3, and your edge is small. [%csl Ra8] }) (18... Qg5 { The queen goes alone, with nothing behind her. } 19. Kh1 Qh5 20. Ba3 { She stands on h5 with no help, and your edge has shrunk. }) 19. Ra2 { He tucks his rook away on a2. What now? } 19... Ra6 { The rook steps out on the sixth row, ready to swing to g6. [%cal Ga6g6][%csl Rg2][%tail] } (19... Rf7 { Also good: the f rook first, the a rook next. } 20. Ba3 Ra6 21. Qb1 Qg5 { Both rooks come into the game, and you are clearly better. [%cal Ga6g6] }) (19... Rf5 { The f rook goes up, and the a rook still waits. } 20. f3 exf3 21. Rxf3 Rxf3 22. gxf3 { His king is open, but your attacking rook got traded. Still clearly better. }) (19... Rf3 { Bold, but the rook gets stuck on f3. } 20. Kh1 Rf7 21. f4 { The rook had to come back, and his f pawn moved up. Still better, but slower. [%csl Rf3] }) (19... Rf6 { This blocks the sixth row for your other rook. } 20. Qb1 Raa6 21. Ba3 { Both rooks now crowd the sixth row. Still better, but clumsy. }) 20. Ba3 Rf5 { The other rook lifts to f5. Both rooks are in the game now, and his bishop on a3 has nothing to bite on. [%csl Gf5,Ga6] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, do nothing well"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/uzv8EH7h"]
[FEN "r1q1r1k1/1p1n1pBp/p3p1p1/P1np4/5P2/3P1NP1/R1P1QPBP/1R4K1 b - - 0 22"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1q1r1k1/1p1n1pBp/p3p1p1/P1np4/5P2/3P1NP1/R1P1QPBP/1R4K1_b_-_-_0_22"]

{ Your bishop has just taken on g7. His pieces on c5, d7 and c8 are crowded together. }
22... Kxg7 { He takes back with the king. What is your plan now? [%csl Rc5,Rd7] } 23. Nd2 { A quiet knight move: it eyes e4 and c4 and gives him nothing to hit. He stays tangled. [%cal Gd2e4,Gd2c4] } (23. c4 { Opening the position is exactly what untangles him. } 23... dxc4 24. Ne5 Nxe5 25. Qxe5+ { His pieces are free again, and you are only a little better. [%csl Gc5] }) (23. Rb4 { The rook climbs to b4 with nothing there to attack. } 23... Qc7 24. Qe1 Nf6 { His knight got out to f6. Still better, but you gained nothing. }) (23. Ne5 { Trades are what the crowded side wants. } 23... Nxe5 24. Qxe5+ Kg8 { The knights are off and his pieces breathe. Better, but less so. }) (23. h4 { A pawn move on the king side does nothing to his knots. } 23... Rb8 24. h5 Qc7 { Your pawn walked to h5, his rook reached b8, and he untangled. Only a little better. }) 23... Nf6 { He brings the d7 knight to f6. What next? } 24. d4 { The pawn hits his knight on c5, and it has no good square. [%cal Gd4c5][%csl Rc5] } (24. c4 { Also good, but the knight on c5 stays a while longer. } 24... h5 25. d4 Nce4 { The pawn reaches d4 a move later. Still better. }) (24. h4 { A king side pawn move does nothing about his knight on c5. } 24... Rd8 25. Qe5 h5 { He gets organised, and you are only a little better. }) (24. Qe3 { The queen eyes c5, and he pushes his d pawn with tempo. } 24... Rb8 25. c4 d4 { His pawn hits your queen on e3, and the plan lost its sting. }) 24... Nce4 { His knight jumps to e4. How do you answer it? } 25. Bxe4 { Your bishop takes the knight on e4 before it can fork your rooks from c3. [%cal Gg2e4] } (25. Nxe4 { Also good: the same trade, knight first. } 25... dxe4 26. Bxe4 Nxe4 27. Qxe4 { A pawn up, with his pieces still scattered. [%csl Ge4] }) (25. Rba1 { This ignores the knight, and it forks your rooks from c3. } 25... Nc3 26. Qe5 Nxa2 { You lost a rook, and he is better. [%csl Ra2] }) (25. c4 { A pawn move with his knight still on e4. } 25... Nc3 26. Qd3 Nxa2 { The knight forked from c3 and took your rook on a2. He is better. }) 25... dxe4 { He takes back with the pawn. } 26. Nxe4 { A pawn up: the knight takes on e4, and his crowded pieces lost a pawn. [%csl Ge4] } (26. Rab2 { The rook steps away, and his queen finds a way in. } 26... Qc3 27. Nxe4 Nxe4 28. Qxe4 Qxa5 { You won e4, he took a5 back, and you are still better. }) (26. Rb4 { The rook goes to b4 while the pawn on e4 is still there to take. } 26... Qc6 27. c4 Red8 { His pawn on e4 is guarded now, and it is about level. }) (26. Nc4 { The knight goes the wrong way. } 26... Nd5 27. Rb3 Qc7 { His knight settles on d5, and you are only slightly better. }) (26. Qxe4 { The queen takes, and his knight on f6 takes her. } 26... Nxe4 { A queen lost for a pawn. [%csl Re4] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, stop his plan"]
