[FEN "2b1k3/1p3pn1/2p1p1p1/2Np3p/3PP3/2P2PB1/1P4PP/4K3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2b1k3/1p3pn1/2p1p1p1/2Np3p/3PP3/2P2PB1/1P4PP/4K3_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ New plan: the weak colour. All his pawns stand on light squares, so the dark squares are yours. Bishop to e5. [%csl Ge5,Gd6,Gf6][%cal Yg3e5] }
1. Be5 { The bishop takes the best dark square, and his knight on g7 has nowhere useful to go. [%csl Ge5,Rg7] } (1. Kf2 { The king moves first, and he frees himself. } 1... dxe4 2. Be5 Nf5 { His knight escaped to f5. You are better, but less so. [%csl Gf5] }) (1. Ke2 { Slow: the bishop has not reached a dark square yet. } 1... Ke7 2. Bh4+ f6 { His f pawn now guards e5 and g5. Still better, but slower. [%cal Rf6e5,Rf6g5] }) (1. Bd6 { The wrong dark square: his king walks past it. } 1... Kd8 2. Be5 Ne8 { You reach e5 a move late, and his knight got to e8. }) (1. exd5 { Opening the position lets his pieces breathe. } 1... exd5 2. Bh4 Ne6 { His knight woke up on e6, and the game is level. [%csl Ge6] }) (1. Nxb7 { The knight grabs a pawn and is taken. } 1... Bxb7 { His bishop takes your knight: a piece for a pawn, and he is better. [%cal Rc8b7] }) 1... Kf8 { He steps his king off the diagonal. Now the knight: a4, then b6. [%cal Yc5a4,Ga4b6] } 2. Na4 { The knight heads for b6, another dark square, where it hits his bishop. [%cal Ga4b6][%csl Gb6] } (2. Bxg7+ { This trades your best piece for his worst. } 2... Kxg7 3. Na4 Bd7 { Still clearly better, but your bishop was the piece to keep. }) (2. Bd6+ { A check from another dark square, then the knight comes. } 2... Kg8 3. Na4 Bd7 { The same plan, one move later. }) (2. Bf6 { You chase his knight, and it walks away. } 2... Ne8 3. e5 Nc7 { The knight got out to c7. Better, but less so. }) (2. Kd2 { The king steps up while the knight waits. } 2... Ne8 3. Na4 Ke7 { His knight escaped to e8, and your plan is slower. }) 2... Bd7 { His bishop guards b5 and c6. Keep coming. [%csl Gb5,Gc6][%cal Ya4b6] } 3. Nb6 { On b6 the knight hits his bishop. Every square in this plan is dark. [%csl Gb6][%cal Gb6d7] } (3. Nc5 { Back to c5, and you go in circles. } 3... Bc8 { His bishop went home too, and nothing changed. [%csl Rc5] }) (3. Bxg7+ { This gives up your best piece. } 3... Kxg7 4. e5 h4 { Still clearly better, only slower. }) (3. Bd6+ { A check first, then the knight goes back. } 3... Kg8 4. Nc5 Bc8 { You are shuffling, though still clearly better. }) 3... Be8 { His bishop hides on e8. Now take f6. [%cal Ye5f6] } 4. Bf6 { Bishop on f6, knight on b6: dark squares his pawns can never attack. His knight on g7 is stuck. That is the weak colour. [%csl Gf6,Gb6,Rg7] } (4. Nc8 { The knight goes hunting on c8 and finds nothing. } 4... b5 5. Bf6 dxe4 { You had to go to f6 anyway. Still clearly better. [%csl Rc8] }) (4. Na4 { Back again: the knight returns to a4, and a move is lost. } 4... dxe4 5. fxe4 f5 { He gets air with f5. Still clearly better, but slower. }) (4. Kf2 { The king moves first, and his pawns start moving. } 4... f5 5. Na4 g5 { His pawns got to f5 and g5. Better, but less so. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, aim the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/DTXSyzN4"]
[FEN "r4rk1/pbpp2pp/1p2q3/5n2/1nPP1B2/2NQ1PPB/PP5P/2KR3R w - - 1 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pbpp2pp/1p2q3/5n2/1nPP1B2/2NQ1PPB/PP5P/2KR3R_w_-_-_1_15"]

{ His king sits on c1, and his light bishop is far away on h3. Which colour is weak around his king? }
15. Qf1 { His queen tucks in on f1. Where does yours go? } 15... Qg6 { Your queen goes to g6, on the light diagonal that runs down to c2, next to his king. [%cal Gg6c2][%csl Gg6] } (15... Qf6 { The queen aims at two pawns, but your knight on b4 is left without help. } 16. a3 g5 17. Be5 { His bishop hits your queen, and the knight on b4 is trapped. You lose a piece. [%csl Rb4] }) (15... g5 { You poke his bishop, and he pokes your knight back. } 16. a3 gxf4 17. axb4 { A piece each, and the game is level. }) (15... Ba6 { The bishop bites on his c4 pawn, and nothing comes of it. } 16. b3 g5 17. a3 { His a pawn kicks your knight on b4, and he is better. [%csl Rb4] }) (15... a5 { You guard the knight, but the queen move was the point. } 16. a3 g5 17. d5 { His centre rolls forward, and he is better. }) (15... Bxf3 { A free pawn? His queen takes back and hits your knight on f5. } 16. Qxf3 g5 17. a3 { Knights on f5 and b4 are both attacked. You lose a piece. [%csl Rb4,Rf5] }) 16. Bxf5 { His bishop takes your knight on f5. How do you take back? } 16... Qxf5 { The queen takes back on f5 and stares down the light diagonal at c2, beside his king. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf5c2] } (16... Rxf5 { The rook takes instead, and your queen stays off the light diagonal. } 17. Rd2 Qf7 18. d5 { His centre rolls forward, and he is a little better. [%csl Rf5] }) 17. Rd2 { His rook guards c2. Where else is he weak on the light squares? [%csl Gc2] } 17... g5 { The pawn kicks his bishop off f4. Your bishop on b7 now looks at f3, guarded only by his queen. [%cal Gg5f4,Gb7f3] } (17... d5 { Solid, but his a pawn kicks your knight. } 18. a3 a5 19. axb4 axb4 { You lost the knight for a pawn, but the a column is open. About level. }) (17... a5 { You guard the knight, and he breaks in the centre. } 18. d5 c6 19. dxc6 Bxc6 { The centre opened, and the game is level. }) (17... Rae8 { The rook waits, and your knight gets kicked anyway. } 18. d5 d6 19. a3 { The knight on b4 must go home, and he is a little better. [%csl Rb4] }) (17... d6 { Slow, and his pawns roll. } 18. h4 a5 19. a3 { His a pawn kicks your knight, and nothing has started on the king side. He is a little better. }) 18. Bxc7 { His bishop takes the pawn on c7 instead of running. } 18... Bxf3 { Your bishop takes f3 and hits his rook on h1. The light squares around his king are yours now. [%cal Gf3h1] } (18... Qa5 { The queen goes after his king, but the f3 pawn was free. } 19. d5 Nxa2+ 20. Kb1 Nxc3+ 21. bxc3 Qxc3 { A wild position, and you are only a little better. }) (18... Qxf3 { This offers the queens off, and his bishop gets a fork. } 19. Qxf3 Rxf3 20. Bd6 { His bishop hits your knight on b4 and your rook on f8, and he is a little better. [%cal Rd6b4,Rd6f8] }) 19. Bd6 { His bishop hits your rook on f8 and your knight on b4. } 19... Bxh1 { Your bishop takes his rook on h1. Now watch the trades. [%csl Gh1][%tail] } (19... Nd3+ { The check gives the knight away. } 20. Qxd3 Qxd3 21. Rxd3 Bxh1 22. Bxf8 Rxf8 { His bishop took your rook, and he is better. }) (19... a5 { This saves the knight, but his bishop takes your rook on f8. } 20. Bxf8 Rxf8 21. a3 Bxh1 22. Qxf5 Rxf5 23. axb4 { Trades everywhere, and he is a little better. }) 20. Qxh1 Qf1+ 21. Qxf1 Rxf1+ 22. Rd1 Rxd1+ 23. Kxd1 Nd3 { The dust settles. You won his rook and the f3 pawn for your bishop, and your knight sits on d3, a light square in his camp. [%csl Gd3] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, the light squares"]
