[FEN "r3n1k1/p1q2pb1/bpp1p1p1/3p2QN/5P2/1P2P3/1BPP2PP/1K5R w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3n1k1/p1q2pb1/bpp1p1p1/3p2QN/5P2/1P2P3/1BPP2PP/1K5R_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ All his pawns stand on light squares. Your move. }
1. Nxg7 { The knight takes on g7, not the bishop: your bishop on b2 stays to rule the long diagonal. Queen to f6 is next. [%cal Gb2g7][%tail] } (1. Bxg7 { The bishop takes, and that was the piece you needed. } 1... Nxg7 2. Nf6+ Kf8 { His king slips to f8, your attack is over, and you are only a little better. [%csl Rf6] }) (1. Qh6 { His bishop takes the queen. } 1... Bxh6 { A whole queen, gone in one move. [%csl Rh6] }) (1. Be5 { Slow: his queen gets out of the way first. } 1... Qd8 2. Nxg7 Qxg5 3. fxg5 Nxg7 { The queens are off, he is a pawn up, and he is better. }) (1. Nf6+ { The knight check gives it away for nothing. } 1... Nxf6 2. Bxf6 Bxf6 3. Qxf6 Re8 { Everything is traded, your attack is over, and he is winning. }) 1... Nxg7 { He takes back with the knight. King to f8 would have lasted longer. [%csl Rg7] } 2. Qf6 Kf8 3. Qxg7+ { Check, and the knight on g7 is yours. Your bishop on b2 guards g7 down the long diagonal, so his king cannot take back. [%cal Gb2g7] } 1-0


