[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/t8L5VOxb"]
[FEN "2r3k1/pq3pp1/5b1p/8/2pr1N2/P5P1/1PQ1RP1P/5RK1 b - - 0 27"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/pq3pp1/5b1p/8/2pr1N2/P5P1/1PQ1RP1P/5RK1_b_-_-_0_27"]

{ New plan: prophylaxis. Ask what he wants, then stop it first. He dreams of your back row and of the squares h5 and f5. [%csl Re8,Rh5,Rf5][%cal Yg7g6] }
27... g6 { One pawn move, three jobs: your king gets g7, and h5 and f5 are taken from his knight and queen. [%csl Gg7,Gh5,Gf5] } (27... Rcd8 { You double rooks, but the back row is still thin. } 28. Rfe1 Qc6 29. Nh5 { His knight lands on h5 and hits your bishop. Only a little better for you. [%csl Rh5] }) (27... Rdd8 { The rook goes back, and the back row worry stays. } 28. h4 Qf3 29. Re3 { His rook chases your queen, and your edge is small. }) (27... Qf3 { The queen goes in alone. } 28. Re3 Qb7 29. Ne2 { She had to go home again, and you are only slightly better. }) (27... h5 { This stops h5 with a pawn, and his knight takes it. } 28. Nxh5 Bg5 { A pawn gone, and he is a little better. [%csl Rh5] }) (27... Rxf4 { A rook for a knight, for no reason. } 28. gxf4 Qf3 29. Qf5 { You gave the exchange, and he is a little better. }) 28. h4 { He grabs space with h4. What does he want next? Cover that too. [%cal Yb7d7] } 28... Qd7 { Queen to d7: she eyes g4 and h3, and she guards the whole back row. [%cal Gd7g4][%csl Gd7] } (28... h5 { This stops h5, and his knight crashes onto g6. } 29. Nxg6 fxg6 30. Qxg6+ Qg7 { Three pawns for the knight, and your king is bare. Still fine, but much messier. [%csl Rg6] }) (28... Kf8 { The king goes the wrong way. } 29. h5 Qd7 30. Rc1 { His pawn reached h5 anyway, and your king is on the wrong side. Better, but less. }) (28... Rdd8 { The rook goes back and gives up the d column. } 29. Rfe1 c3 30. bxc3 { You gave the c pawn for nothing, and your edge is small. }) (28... Rd6 { Passive: the back row is still loose. } 29. Rfe1 c3 30. Re8+ Rxe8 31. Rxe8+ Kg7 { His rook reached e8 with check. Only a little better. [%csl Re8] }) 29. Rfe1 { He doubles rooks on the e column. Now think about your king. [%cal Yg8g7] } 29... Kg7 { The king steps to g7, so no back row tricks are left. The h pawn push is yours now, not his. [%csl Gg7][%cal Gh6h5] } (29... Kf8 { The wrong square: his knight crashes in on g6. } 30. Nxg6+ fxg6 31. Qxg6 Qf7 32. Qxh6+ { He got three pawns for the knight, and your king has no cover. Only a little better. [%csl Rg6] }) (29... Qg4 { The queen goes alone. } 30. Re4 Qd7 { She had to come back to d7, and a tempo is gone. }) (29... Rf8 { A rook move when the king was the point. } 30. h5 g5 31. Ng2 { His h pawn reached h5, and the game is level. }) (29... Rxf4 { A rook for a knight again. } 30. gxf4 Qg4+ 31. Kf1 Qh3+ { Your queen checks forever, and it is a draw. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, what does he threaten"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/fLvrdyeq"]
[FEN "7k/3p2pp/4p3/8/2Qp2P1/4b3/2P2rPP/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7k/3p2pp/4p3/8/2Qp2P1/4b3/2P2rPP/6K1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ His rook on f2 has a discovered check loaded behind it. What is he threatening? [%csl Rf2,Re3] }
1. Kh1 { Step off the diagonal first. The discovered check is dead, and your queen is free to work. [%csl Gh1] } (1. Qa6 { You attack first, and his rook takes on c2 with discovered check. } 1... Kg8 2. h4 Rxc2+ { A pawn gone with check, and the game is level. [%csl Rc2] }) (1. Qd3 { The queen walks into the discovered check. } 1... Rd2+ 2. Kf1 Rxd3 { His rook moved with check, then took your queen. [%csl Rd3] }) (1. c3 { A pawn move, and his d pawn attacks your queen. } 1... d5 2. Qxd4 Rf3+ 3. Qxe3 Rxe3 { The discovered check came anyway, and your queen fell on e3. [%csl Re3] }) (1. Qxd4 { A free pawn? Look again. } 1... Bxd4 { His bishop on e3 guarded d4. A queen for a pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (1. Qc8+ { A check of your own, and he blocks with a discovered check. } 1... Rf8+ { His rook blocks on f8 with check and attacks your queen on c8. She is lost. [%csl Rc8] }) (1. h3 { Air for your king, but the discovered check is still loaded. } 1... Rxc2+ { Discovered check, and his rook on c2 now attacks your queen on c4. [%csl Rc2] }) 1... h6 { He makes air for his king. What does he want next? } 2. g3 { One pawn move, two jobs: f4 is taken from his bishop, and g2 is free for your king. [%csl Gg2,Gf4] } (2. h3 { Air for the king, but his bishop reaches f4. } 2... Bf4 3. Qd3 Kg8 { His bishop reached f4, and the game is level. [%csl Rf4] }) (2. h4 { The pawn goes one square too far. } 2... Bf4 3. g3 Bxg3 { His bishop took the pawn on g3, and it is level. }) (2. Qc8+ { A check that gains nothing. } 2... Kh7 3. g3 Rf1+ { His rook is active on f1, and the game is level. }) (2. Qa6 { The queen hunts pawns. } 2... Kg8 3. Qc8+ Kh7 { His king is tucked away, and the position is equal. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, stop it and push"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/q6pj3WWp"]
[FEN "3r1rk1/1ppnq1p1/2n1bp1p/p1b1p3/2N1P2N/2P1B1P1/PP2QPBP/3R1RK1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r1rk1/1ppnq1p1/2n1bp1p/p1b1p3/2N1P2N/2P1B1P1/PP2QPBP/3R1RK1_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ He wants his knight on g6, hitting your queen and rook at once. Find a move that stops it and helps your own plan. }
1... Qf7 { Queen to f7: she covers g6 and lines up behind your bishop on e6, aiming at his knight on c4. [%cal Gf7g6,Ge6c4] } (1... Kh7 { The king steps aside, and he trades off your good bishop. } 2. Bxc5 Qxc5 3. Ne3 { His knight settles on e3, and the game is level. [%csl Ge3] }) (1... Bxc4 { Taking first lets his queen take back with check. } 2. Qxc4+ Qf7 3. Qxf7+ Rxf7 4. Rxd7 { Your knight on d7 was left hanging. A piece down. [%csl Rd7] }) (1... Bxe3 { You give up the bishop, and his knight takes back. } 2. Nxe3 Qf7 3. Bf3 { His knight on e3 is fine, your bishop is gone, and he is a little better. }) (1... b6 { Slow, and the knight jump is still coming. } 2. Bf3 Qe8 3. b3 { The knight jump is only delayed, and he is a little better. }) (1... Rfe8 { The rook moves, and your bishop gets traded off. } 2. Bf3 b5 3. Bxc5 Qxc5 { Your good bishop was traded, and he is a little better. }) 2. b3 { He guards the knight with a pawn. What now? } 2... Bxc4 { Your bishop takes the knight. His pawn must take back, and then the pawn on c4 is loose. [%cal Gb3c4] } (2... b6 { You wait, and he tidies up. } 3. Bc1 Ne7 4. Bf3 { He untangled for free, and it is level. }) (2... Bxe3 { The wrong bishop trades first. } 3. Nxe3 Nb6 4. Bf3 { His knight found e3, and the game is level. }) (2... Ba7 { The bishop steps back. } 3. Bxa7 Nxa7 4. Bf3 { The bishops came off and his knight on c4 is untouched. He is a little better. }) (2... b5 { The pawn hits the knight, and he trades bishops first. } 3. Bxc5 Nxc5 4. Ne3 { His knight jumped to e3, safe and central. He is a little better. }) 3. bxc4 { He takes back with the pawn. What is next? } 3... Bxe3 { Your bishop takes his bishop, and his queen must take back. Then the c4 pawn has no guard. [%cal Gf7c4] } (3... b6 { Slow, and your bishop on c5 is still attacked. } 4. Bc1 Ne7 5. Bh3 { His bishop swung to h3, and the game is level. }) (3... Ba7 { The bishops come off anyway, and c4 stays safe. } 4. Bxa7 Nxa7 5. Bf3 { The c4 pawn is guarded now, and he is a little better. }) (3... Ncb8 { The knight goes all the way home. } 4. Bf3 Bxe3 5. Bh5 { His bishop hits your queen from h5, and he is a little better. }) 4. Qxe3 { He takes back with the queen. What is loose now? } 4... Qxc4 { A pawn up, and his light squares are full of holes. [%csl Gc4] } (4... b6 { You wait, and he guards the pawn. } 5. Qe2 Nc5 6. Nf5 { The c4 pawn is safe now. Only slightly better for you. }) (4... Ne7 { The knight moves, and the pawn runs to c5. } 5. Bh3 Nb6 6. c5 { The pawn escaped to c5, and it is level. }) (4... Nb6 { The knight hits c4, and the pawn runs. } 5. c5 Na4 6. Rd5 { The pawn escaped, his rook is active on d5, and it is level. }) 0-1


