{ He is lining up his rooks on the d column, facing your queen on d6. }
17. Rad1 { Both his rooks now stand on the d column. } 17... Qc7 { Queen to c7: off the d column, and guarding f7 and e5 at once. [%csl Gc7][%cal Gc7f7,Gc7e5][%tail] } (17... cxd4 { Opening the position while cramped helps his rooks. } 18. Rxd4 Qc7 19. Ne5 { His rook on d4 and knight on e5 pour in, and he is a little better. [%csl Rd4] }) (17... Rf8 { A rook move, and your queen still stands on the d column. } 18. Ne5 a6 19. Qf4 { His pressure never let up, and he is a little better. }) (17... Qc6 { Off the column, but f7 is loose. } 18. Ne5 Qc7 19. Qf4 { You went to c7 anyway, two moves late. Level. }) (17... Qb8 { Off the column, but too far back. } 18. Ne5 Qc7 19. Qf4 { Back to c7 a move later, with his knight already on e5. Level. }) (17... Qe7 { Also playable: off the column, and f7 stays covered. } 18. Ne5 Qc7 { The queen ends up on c7 anyway. }) (17... Ne4 { The knight jumps in, and his queen takes it. } 18. Qxe4 { A whole knight, gone. [%csl Re4] }) 18. h3 h6 19. Rd3 cxd4 20. Rxd4 Re8 { Your rook on e8 finishes the tidy-up. The queen left the d column, f7 and e5 are guarded, and his doubled rooks hit nothing. The game is level. [%csl Ge8] } 0-1
